{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2163 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a6389d33c2c8b6cbc92a99482a8605b9e15e85e8195b2e104df9378d69352f3f" :edb/db-before-t 2162 :edb/db-after-t 2163 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535475 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:52:55.930Z" 13194139535475 true] [17592186063937 1100 "A circular oil spillage is being cleaned up by a team using specialized cleaning equipment. If the area of the circle is *decreasing* at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^2/\\text{h}$, what is the rate at which the radius is decreasing at the instant when the radius is $2\\,\\text{m}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/287/Source/Images/774e528ccec7ab87522854904beb1de5.png)" 13194139535475 false] [17592186063937 1100 "A circular oil spillage is being cleaned up by a team using specialized cleaning equipment. If the area of the circle is *decreasing* at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^2/\\text{h}$, what is the rate at which the radius is decreasing at the instant when the radius is $2\\,\\text{m}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/d769569ed7854f4d6eb085853b51eb0e5aab0e14cfc3bcdf1e5a480d996ccc9e.png)" 13194139535475 true] [17592186063937 1145 "In this situation, we want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nat the moment when $r=2$, and we are also told that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -6\\pi\n$$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dr}(πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 2πr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dA}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\n$$\n\nand $r=2$, and solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6π &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 2π(2) \\\\\n- 6π &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 4π \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is decreasing at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nmeter per hour." 13194139535475 false] [17592186063937 1145 "In this situation, we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=2,$ and we are also told that  $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -6\\pi.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm dA}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (2) \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ meter per hour." 13194139535475 true] [17592186063938 1145 "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A = \\pi r^2,$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA =  \\pi r^2 =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\pi  \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186063938 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nthe relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2 = \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{C^{2}}{4π}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dC}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dC}(\\frac{C^{2}}{4π}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dC}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{C}{2π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dC}{dt} &= \\frac{dC}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{C}{2π} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{C}{2π} \\\\\nC &= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2πr &= π \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 false] [17592186063939 1100 "Air is leaking from a spherical balloon at the rate of $500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s}$. How fast is the radius of the balloon decreasing when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/287/Source/Images/24a7b7fce6675f45e6355f3472d0186a.png)" 13194139535475 false] [17592186063939 1100 "Air is leaking from a spherical balloon at the rate of $500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius of the balloon decreasing when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/460fd4059be2c655e01b89db2738225a50578b5c76bbe048f83312f0bff655ec.png)" 13194139535475 true] [17592186063939 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nand we want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nr=10\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(V) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{4}{3}πr^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dr}(\\frac{4}{3}πr^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 4πr^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi\n$$\n\nand $r=10$, and solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-500π &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 4π(10)^{2} \\\\\n- 500π &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot 400π \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -1.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the balloon is decreasing at a rate of $1.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}$." 13194139535475 false] [17592186063939 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}$ when $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi$ and $r=10,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-500\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (10)^{2} \\\\ -500\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 400\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the balloon is decreasing at a rate of $1.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186064291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351099 13194139535475 true] [17592186064291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351106 13194139535475 true] [17592186064291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351126 13194139535475 true] [17592186064291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351140 13194139535475 true] [17592186064292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351092 13194139535475 true] [17592186064292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351133 13194139535475 true] [17592186064292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351137 13194139535475 true] [17592186064292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351154 13194139535475 true] [17592186064292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351161 13194139535475 true] [17592186064293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351119 13194139535475 true] [17592186064293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351129 13194139535475 true] [17592186064293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351144 13194139535475 true] [17592186064293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351148 13194139535475 true] [17592186064293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351151 13194139535475 true] [17592186064574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186061955 13194139535475 false] [17592186064574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351113 13194139535475 true] [17592186064574 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535475 true] [17592186064574 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/d4c0faafff52f414c30a4da32f9f0ba3520ef88d7c8bebe5c12a7ccea3109d17.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5\\text{ cm}?$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186064574 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/287/Source/Images/q-2547.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40πcm^{2}/min$. How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5cm$?" 13194139535475 false] [17592186064574 1145 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=5,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi $ and $r=5,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (5) \\\\ 40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 10\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $4$ centimeters per minute." 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351093 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1089 "q-115919" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1100 "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three-fifths of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1113 #uuid "e4a753a6-f7b9-5a6d-be3f-8697734caaa2" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351092 1145 "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351094 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351095 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351096 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351097 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351098 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1041 "selection" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351096 13194139535475 true] [17592186351093 1110 #uuid "7dd1e494-0d7d-5d5c-8bd1-bb9576ef2587" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351094 1034 "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351094 1111 #uuid "a364baef-5f61-57e1-beb5-a0fdca64582f" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351095 1034 "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351095 1111 #uuid "13faf97a-ac74-5300-ab04-adfff1bcf9d7" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351096 1034 "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351096 1111 #uuid "789f57b5-c846-5ce6-ba04-e38ac6562e64" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351097 1034 "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351097 1111 #uuid "b682e1dd-880a-5171-8fef-810a6e2f91f5" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351098 1034 "\\frac{1}{5\\pi }" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351098 1111 #uuid "e48dbf8b-7038-5b39-b444-4332d86a0587" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351100 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1089 "q-116650" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1113 #uuid "5772907f-89d6-59e3-8211-18050e552f56" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351099 1145 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\