t when the radius is $10\\,\\text{mm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe area of the ink blot is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351126 1113 #uuid "5f12f32c-9ad0-541c-9d58-9375624fa3eb" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351126 1145 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=10,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=10\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (10)=20\\pi \\text{ mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi $ and $C=20\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{20\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area is increasing at a rate of $40\\pi {\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351128 13194139535475 true] [17592186351127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535475 true] [17592186351127 1041 "field-1" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351128 13194139535475 true] [17592186351127 1110 #uuid "5c41a92c-8cfe-52cd-865e-f2f4128913e9" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351128 1034 "40\\pi" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351128 1111 #uuid "e1e9e08e-f8c4-56ab-b399-fa19ad8cb6aa" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351130 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1089 "q-315860" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1113 #uuid "66dac43f-c2e2-5767-925b-2e5479d07e2b" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351129 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351131 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351132 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1041 "field-1" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351132 13194139535475 true] [17592186351130 1110 #uuid "d56df562-06cf-5859-ade6-71d56493f1cd" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351131 1034 "4\\pi" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351131 1111 #uuid "6ace003c-0c0a-5479-be83-a5c3e50e7ccf" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351132 1034 "8\\pi" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351132 1111 #uuid "7655f309-0c8f-5637-adf2-c9df7010bd2f" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351134 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1089 "q-344661" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1100 "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to nine times the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the diameter $D$ of the circle at that moment?\n\n$D=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1113 #uuid "bc1e09a0-52d9-593f-80f3-e2133be81a8c" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351133 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $D$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =18\\pi  \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =2\\cdot 9 \\\\ D & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351135 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351136 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1041 "field-1" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351136 13194139535475 true] [17592186351134 1110 #uuid "4ed5e393-37f8-5a8f-a7d8-3a647f3b2806" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351135 1034 "9" 13194139535475 true] [17592186351135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535475 true] [17592186351135 1111 #uuid "a770eb6